yesterday you took part in a panel  discussion here at the fdth council  event on do we really need fiber before  you give me your points tell me a little  bit about ekta the organization that you  represent  actor is the european competitive  telecommunications association brian  and  we represent the challenger operators of  europe so really the challengers to the  former monopolist incumbent operators  who typically provide competitive and  innovative broadband and triple play  services  they are also  the so-called mobile entrance  and thirdly  business service providers often you're  often offering services to pan-european  multinationals for example okay so what  were your you know what was your input  to the session today  i think it is very important to bear in  mind that competition is really the best  way for europe to explore its digital  future and therefore i think the path  towards that digital future  will actually really define the outcome  so i think from this perspective it is  extremely important that regulation that  policy actually supports in focuses on  enabling competition and enabling  competitors to invest efficiently in  their own networks  and to compete and to innovate  yes i would imagine that your membership  therefore are very very much in favor of  this concept of building open networks  indeed  of course challenger operators  tend to provide  access to others when they build their  own networks but equally  when you think about fixed broadband  networks you should think about them as  being very similar to for example  electricity lines or water pipes or or  gas pipes so that you can never really  create  effectively competition by expecting  that there will be you know four or five  of those lines going to  into one single home so therefore  actually having access to those very  last miles of the fixed broadband  network  is the best and proven way to actually  create competition  to create affordable prices for end  users  and and innovative products  and and therefore of course i think it's  very important that regulation going  forward  refocuses on making sure that the  broadband markets when it comes to very  high speeds often supported by fiber  are competitive  and they really deliver affordability  innovation to the end user yes now of  course there's going to be a telecoms  review coming up by the eu so what would  you like to them to see  like you to see them do  um i think that a key challenge and i  would argue that a key task of the  revision of the eu's telecoms framework  is really to make sure  that in in the next generation of  broadband  the market will remain effectively  competitive or rather it will become  effectively competitive uh there is a  very high risk and we see developments  in this regard today  that europe would slide back on  competition indeed in some of the  high-speed broadband markets  the incumbent operators are really  regaining market shares so you can see  up to 70 percent retail market share  that is very worrying and that should be  a worrying sign so i think it is of  utmost importance that when it comes to  next generation broadband networks  regulation focuses on creating  competition not letting europe slide  back and also that regulation with the  so-called physical access products like  unbundling in sub loop unbundling  actually ensures that  alternative competitive operators can  invest in their own networks and compete  effectively  with the former monopolies or other  networks  thank you very much for talking to us  thank you brian  so a call there from actor for more and  more competition in the marketplace
 

